{"builder":"Goal: make one pull request that meets both of these conditions:\n1. \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n2. \"npm test passes\"\n\nWhat the code does now: `src/config.js` has `loadConfig(path)`, which returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. Invalid JSON makes it throw a SyntaxError. `src/app.js` merges the result over `DEFAULTS`, catches any error, prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1. `README.md` describes that behaviour. `test/config.test.js` has a test called `bad JSON throws`, which expects a SyntaxError.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, change `loadConfig(path)` so that a failure in `JSON.parse` is caught and the function returns `{}`. Catch only the parse step. Do not wrap `readFileSync` in the same try, so a missing or unreadable file still throws as it does now. Valid JSON must still be returned unchanged.\n2. In `test/config.test.js`, replace the `bad JSON throws` test with one that checks `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns a value deep-equal to `{}`. Keep the `reads a config` test.\n3. Update `README.md` so it no longer says invalid JSON stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1. Describe what really happens now: with invalid JSON, `loadConfig` returns `{}` and the app starts with its defaults. Leave the `src/app.js` catch for other errors, such as a missing file.\n4. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure it exits 0 with no failing tests.\n5. In the PR description, say plainly that with this change a malformed config.json no longer stops the app with an error. It now starts silently on the defaults, and the user is not told which file is broken or where. Ask the reviewers to confirm this is the behaviour they want.","verifier":"Run these checks at the PR head, from the repository root, in this order. There is one check per expect line.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun:\n```\nd=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/bad.json\"; printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > \"$d/good.json\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig(process.argv[1]); console.log(JSON.stringify(r)); console.log(JSON.stringify(loadConfig(process.argv[2])));\" \"$d/bad.json\" \"$d/good.json\"; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\nPASS: there is no SyntaxError or stack trace. The first output line is exactly `{}`, the second is `{\"port\":9000}`, and the last line is `exit=0`. Also open `src/config.js` and confirm that `loadConfig` is defined there and that the `{}` comes from catching the `JSON.parse` error.\nFAIL: the command throws, prints a SyntaxError, exits non-zero, prints anything other than `{}` for the bad file, or does not return the parsed object for the good file. It also fails if the behaviour comes from somewhere other than `loadConfig` in `src/config.js`.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: `npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"`\nPASS: the last line is `exit=0` and the TAP summary shows `# fail 0`.\nFAIL: the exit code is not 0, or any test fails or is cancelled.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS or FAIL):\n- Check whether `loadConfig` on a path that does not exist still throws. Run `node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; loadConfig('/nonexistent/config.json')\"`. Ideally it throws ENOENT and does not return `{}`. Report what you see.\n- Check that `test/config.test.js` no longer expects a SyntaxError for bad JSON and has a test for the `{}` result.\n- Check that `README.md` no longer says invalid JSON stops the app with exit 1. With a bad file, `node src/app.js <bad.json>` is now expected to print `listening on 8080`.\n- Note in your report that the app now starts silently on the defaults when config.json is malformed. The user is not told which file is broken or where."}